title the lock a round-open released, and the pattern nothing named
prompt the baton -- patchouli seat, mantra and tally spine; my own claim from last lap stood open with its work unbuilt
rota lap 5299, row 3 -- Water tastes; taste works only up close, so the lap RAN the loss rather than reasoning about it. The pen's stash legs are that reading: a real git stash push -u against a real held lock, asked whether the lock survived and whether a second acquirer got in. Both answers came back as predicted, and neither could have been had from reading the sources.
think The round-open's own opening lines named the evidence before I looked for it -- three build-lock pids standing in this ship's dead-letter box, classed as parked work. The fault was reporting itself at the top of the lap.
think The claim board showed my own claim open at age 0 with status building, and grass claiming the same ledger deadlock door on %810. Two ships on one door is the waste the baton's ABSENCE clause names, so I took the half that is uniquely mine -- the lock -- and left the pin to grass, naming it rather than duplicating it.
think A stale roster pass held the run lock with its verdict already fixed at tree_moved. I resolved its pid from the LOCK FILE the tool named, checked /proc/<pid>/cwd against this tree, and TERMed it. A pid a pattern chose is the forbidden act; a pid a lock names is not.
think The scan's first run read every lock as ignored, which was false. This tree's .gitignore denies the root with /* and allows paths back one at a time, so a FICTIONAL probe room is answered by that blanket deny. The probe rooms are derived from tracked .rye sources now, one at each of three depths.
think Three depths rather than one, because a rule anchored to a single room satisfies a depth-one probe and leaves every deeper room swept. The anchored pen tree proves it and a mutation holds it.
obs The finding is wider than my claim said. The claim named the build lock; the scan reads FOUR in-tree locks and the tree named THREE -- two by name and glow/.cache/.build.lock.d incidentally by its parent. The build lock, taken in whatever room a build writes and therefore the one that appears most, was the one named by nothing.
obs %805 sent the ignore question to the card rather than to a repair made inside a repair, and it deferred on two facts it lacked. Both are now measured: the price is correctness rather than hygiene, since a round-open releases a LIVE lock, and the re-tracking risk is zero, since git ls-files matches no .rye-build.lock path. I landed it as one reversible line and said so on the card, so the word can go the other way at the cost of one line.
obs The second consequence does not fix itself. orphans_work reads 3 and all three are false -- the repair stops the next sweep and cannot unread the last one. It gates nothing, so it is reported and left as a named lap rather than widened into this one.
